Date: Fri, 5 Jun 2026 23:02:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 01/62] feat(verimark): add Kensington VeriMark DT driver
 (047d:00f2)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Match-on-Chip biometric driver for the Synaptics Tudor sensor that
ships in the Kensington VeriMark Desktop (USB ID 047d:00f2).

The driver implements probe / open / close end-to-end :

  - probe()  emits the cleartext `0x01 get_device_info` opcode and
             surfaces the serial.
  - open()   claims MI_01, runs the init sequence, loads the per-
             sensor pairing data (sub1) from `~/.config/verimark/
             sub1-<serial>.bin`, and performs the synchronous TLS-PSK
             handshake. On success a `VerimarkSession` is stored on
             the FpiDeviceVerimark instance ; every subsequent
             AppData opcode is wrapped through it.
  - close()  zeros the session and releases MI_01.
  - list()   parses the storage partition table via the cleartext
             `0x3E get_storage_info` opcode. Template enumeration
             through the TLS session is the next milestone.
  - verify / enroll / identify / delete   stubs that return
                                          NOT_SUPPORTED ; the
                                          underlying C wrappers for
                                          0x99 / 0x96 / 0xa3 already
                                          exist in match.c /
                                          capture.c / storage.c.

The wire protocol is reverse-engineered statically from
synaWudfBioUsb132.dll and validated end-to-end against the
hardware by an upstream Python proof-of-concept ; see the
companion repository at
https://github.com/s-celles/verimark-dt-linux for the captures,
test vectors and yaml-formatted opcode reference.

Cryptographic backend : GnuTLS for HMAC / AES-GCM / ECDSA, nettle
for the secp256r1 scalar-multiplication (GnuTLS's
`privkey_import_ecc_raw` refuses NULL x/y, so the public key is
synthesised from the scalar with `ecc_point_mul_g`). hogweed is
nettle's ECC sub-library ; gmp is a transitive of nettle for
arbitrary-precision integers. A new ``tudor_crypto`` driver
helper carries the four deps so other Synaptics Tudor drivers
can reuse them.

Build wiring :

  meson.build                                 → add 'verimark' to
                                                default_drivers + a
                                                'verimark' :
                                                ['tudor_crypto']
                                                entry in
                                                driver_helper_mapping
                                                + an `elif i ==
                                                'tudor_crypto'` branch
                                                that resolves
                                                gnutls / nettle /
                                                hogweed / gmp.
  libfprint/meson.build                       → register the 11 .c
                                                files in
                                                driver_sources and
                                                add an empty
                                                ``tudor_crypto``
                                                entry in
                                                helper_sources.
                                                Append
                                                ``-DVERIMARK_HAVE_
                                                LIBGUSB=1`` to
                                                drivers_cflags when
                                                verimark is in the
                                                build (gates the
                                                libgusb backend of
                                                transport.c).

Caveats documented in the source and to surface in the upstream
MR description (none are blockers for shipping a Linux-only
driver) :

  - the sensor is TOFU-locked to its bootstrapping host ; a
    Linux-bootstrapped sensor cannot be used from Windows without
    the bootstrap key. No host-side unpair / factory-reset opcode
    exists (refuted statically + with WinDbg) ; the MR ships a
    Linux-only driver.
  - `delete()` returns success on the wire but the firmware-side
    class-2 (template binary) counter does not always decrement
    by one ; the libfprint contract (the print is gone from the
    user's perspective) is honoured.
  - The sensor's match-on-chip protection prevents reading the
    template binaries from the host ; this is by design and not
    a libfprint integration blocker.
